ng  the x axis when you look up and down and  along the y axis as you look  side-to-side these rotations should be  set such in such a way that they're  always facing us when you call this  function you'll get back three numbers  that represent which direction you're  looking at in 3d space you can think of  x and z as the direction that you're  looking at side to side and y represents  whether you're looking up or down or  something in between  here's a diagram that might help explain  what's going on here so in this example  the camera is facing due north so when  you're facing to north  x is 0 that is 1 when south when you're  facing south x is 0 and that is negative  1 the issue here is that regardless of  whichever direction we're facing the  stickers are always spawning up where  the camera is located what we want is  two things  position position the sticker where the  camera is facing and rotate the sticker  in such a way that's facing the camera  we can do this by taking the inputs of  XYZ coordinates of the camera and its  direction we then use those numbers to  upload the correct X&amp;Y rotations of the  stickers for me it's hard to think about  how to take three directional values and  output two rotational values to make  this simpler let's just consider the  case where the when the cameras  Direction is pointed at the northern  half of the scene which is when Zed is  greater than or equal to zero once we  sold it for that case we can just flip  the Y value for when Zed is lesser than  zero to get the mirror image this gets  us the correct rotation so in this  diagram you can see that the rotation  remains the same whether you're facing  due north or due south but when you're  facing North East the rotation is a  mirror image of when you're facing  southeast same thing for north west and  southwest  after playing around with the inspector  we can determine that the directional y  values of negative 1 and positive 1  should map to the x-axis rotation of 0  through negative 180 so to do this we  can create a linear scale with the  appropriate domain and range we do the  same thing for the x axis rotation which  takes in the directional X value when we  detect that the Z value of the cameras  to camera Direction is lesser than 0 we  just multiply the scaled value by  negative 1 to flip the sign so this  creates a mirror image in terms of the  rotation and after he makes the  calculation we set the rotation values  as a reminder this is how the stickers  used to spawn they would always face up  no matter where you are or which way  you're facing with the changes you can  see that the stickers are always facing  us no matter which way we face so so far  we've covered how to position the  sticker correctly and set the rotation  such that it always faces us next we'll  be figuring out how to launch the  sticker using a physics engine a frame  doesn't come with physics out of the box  so this means that it doesn't have  things like gravity collision events and  dynamic motion thankfully there's a  great package out there called a frame  physics system it's built on top of  Canon js' which is a WebGL physics  engine before it can get started with  adding motion to our scene we should  talk about the concept of bodies so  bodies are things that the physics  engine can interact with that surround  an entity in the frame physics there are  two kinds of bodies dynamic bodies and  Static bodies dynamic bodies moved  freely they have mass and react when  they collide with another object they  also fall if gravity's is enabled static  bodies are fixed or animate in a fixed  way so when they collide with other  objects the static bodies are unaffected  if reduce at the sofa is dynamic body  for example  so if ya fruit is that the the sofa is  dynamic body for example the stuff I'll  go flying is he put stickers on it and  so in this model you want our furniture  and walls to be static bodies and we  want and so when the sticker collides  with them they shouldn't move like like  this and we want our stickers to be some  blend of both we want the stickers to be  dynamic as they fly through the air but  when they had something they should  stick and remain stuck with this in mind  let's start putting in motion to the  scene as you did previously we need to  get the vector of the direction the  direction of the camera spacing so we  can launch the sticker in that direction  by directly setting the velocity on the  stickers body now keep in mind that we  do this after the physics body of the  element has loaded which happens after  the initial rendering so at this point  we've managed to generate stickers in  the correct position and launch them in  the direction that we're looking at  however we can see that the sticker is  simply just bouncing off the furniture  in walls instead of actually sticking to  them and this because the stickers  remain dynamic bodies after they collide  with the static body so to fix this  we'll be creating a collision event  listeners on these stickers when the  sticker collides with an object we can  check to see what it collided with if it  collided with the static body then we  know it's either the walls or the  furniture and that should it should  stick at that point we can have it  remain stationary by turning into a  static body so here you can see that as  soon as the sticker hits the static body  it turns into a static body itself so  that makes it stop moving and looks like  make it makes it look like it's sticking  to the sofa in the previous clip you'll  notice that the stickers don't stick  perfectly on the surface of the models  and this is because of how the physics  engine interprets models they don't  respect the curvature of the leaves of  this plan or the individual cushions on  the sofa and this is because the physics  engine interprets the models as  primitive shapes rather than the complex  models that they are this is to make the  engine more performant you can see how  the physics engine sees each of the  models to the debug mode so you can see  here that the  the engine sees the plant as a box  instead of the individual leaves or the  pot not a citizen now there's a slight  bug with the physics engine when you  create a custom non primitive entity and  then you rotate it the entity is rotated  but it's physics body is not so as you  can see here the physics body of the of  the doors orthogonal to its actual like  rendered thing so we can fix this by  mightily creating the desired physics  body first you can create a primitive  that most closely fits your model so for  the store we can create a box element  that's a static body and then place it  like so then you make it invisible and  then you're done you fake that physics  body and no one will be the wiser  I'd like to close out with a demo of  what the actual final part looks like  the demo is in it's obviously not a  perfect recreation of the obliteration  room but I think we managed to get  pretty far with the time that we had  now this quick demo we've covered a few  things generating a scene adding in  models and lighting dynamically  generating elements adding in a physics  engine and interaction and modifying  bodies now this should this barely  scratches the surface of what you can do  in VR some of my favorite VR pieces are  tilt brush paintings like this painting  of the countryside in Austria you can do  this you can see that this accomplishes  well you can't do with just regular  painting it's actually more like a  sculpture than a painting it really  conveys depth and has little details  that you couldn't do in a painting so  you can see under the water there that  there are fish swimming in the water and  you can see the underneath the boat and  inside the house and unlike a sculpture  though you can download it and endlessly  remix up to make your own then others  can download your work and remix it and  so on because it's digital isn't only  replicable because it's VR it can be  meaningfully interactive so I encourage  all of you to go out there and make  something at 360 video game a 360 video  a game an interesting tilt brush  painting or the simplest scene in a  frame VR is still uncharted territory  and everyone's soul just trying to  figure things out embrace the novelty of  it all  so thank you to everyone for coming to  my talk and thank you to jazz comp you  guys for having me and a special thank  you to  Vidia Nova sitting right there for  helping me a lot with this talk and you  can find the demo that I showed in this  role over here and this my handle on  Twitter where I'll be tweeting out a  link to my slice and there later today  thank you  [Applause]


